{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Moses diligently sought, etc.</b> I say that Moses had not firmly decided in his mind to permit it for eating, and the proof is from the fact that he did not state its law to them as he stated to them regarding the meal-offering (Leviticus 10:12): ״קְחוּ אֶת הַמִּנְחָה״, etc. — for if it had been in his mind straightforwardly to permit it, he should have said: ״קְחוּ אֶת הַחַטָּאת״. What will you say — that he stated the law of the meal-offering and thought there was no difference between them at all, and because of the great simplicity the matter had for him he did not need to state it? If so, he should not have needed to seek, for from where did the suspicion enter him to investigate about it — what had happened to it? Rather, it is certain that he said only what was simple to him concerning the meal-offering, which is קָדְשֵׁי שָׁעָה (sancta of the moment), and from it [he reasoned] to the goat of Nahshon and the goat of the women, which were also קָדְשֵׁי שָׁעָה; but the goat of the new moon, which is קָדְשֵׁי דוֹרוֹת (sancta for all generations), the matter still stood before him in doubt, and he was deliberating in his mind whether to permit it likewise or not, and he investigated what had happened to it. Therefore he doubled the expression <b>darosh darash</b> — one for its law and one for itself, what had happened to it: ״וְהִנֵּה״, etc. And he was angry that they had done a thing on their own initiative, since even he himself still did not yet know what he would command them to do. And although he said at the end: ״תֹּאכְלוּ אוֹתָהּ כַּאֲשֶׁר צִוֵּיתִי״ — meaning: you should have been more inclined to compare it to the meal-offering, which I commanded to eat, and not to burn it without reason, the opposite of what I commanded regarding the meal-offering. And in Torat Kohanim they said: ״דָּרֹשׁ דָּרַשׁ״ — for what reason was it burned, and for what reason were these not eaten? And upon reflection the matter is far-removed, for it was only after the investigations that the burning became known, so how could he investigate beforehand why it was burned? And there is a way to settle this." "<b>The ones who remained, saying.</b> The verse's saying <b>leimor</b> may be explained according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 13): ״הַנּוֹתָרִים״ — Rabbi Pinchas said: woe that you remained! — end quote. And this is the verse's saying <b>הַנּוֹתָרִים לֵאמֹר</b> — meaning, the ones who remained are to say something else, and that is the statement of Rabbi Pinchas. And in Torat Kohanim they said: ״לֵאמֹר״ — he said to them: answer me concerning my words, meaning he permitted them to answer, that they would not refrain out of seeing that he was angry with them. It further intends, according to the statement of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 73) regarding the verse ״צֹאן לָבָן הַנּוֹתָרוֹת״, which is an expression of diminishment. And this is the verse's saying <b>הַנּוֹתָרִים לֵאמֹר</b> — as if it said לֵאמֹר הַנּוֹתָרִים, meaning: he called them in his utterance נוֹתָרִים — an expression of diminishment."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:03.259684391Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}